== Variants ==

=== Brouillard ===
[[Image:Bugatti Brouillard.jpg|thumb|Brouillard at Chantilly Arts & Elegance Richard Mille in 2026]]
In 2025, Bugatti revealed a one-off modification of the Mistral called Brouillard, named after one of Ettore Bugatti's horses. As opposed to the Mistral, it is not a roadster but a closed coupé. The Brouillard is the first car in Bugatti's so called "Programme Solitaire", an exclusive programme dedicated to creating special one-off versions of existing models. The Brouillard uses the {{cvt|1,600|PS|kW hp PS|0|order=out}} {{cvt|7993|cc|cuin L|0|abbr=on}} quad-turbocharged W16 engine from the [[Bugatti Chiron|Chiron Super Sport]], as well as an all-wheel-drive setup.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Pattni |first=Vijay |date=2025-08-07 |title=Surprise! This is the one-off, 1,578bhp Bugatti Brouillard |url=https://www.topgear.com/car-news/first-look/surprise-one-1578bhp-bugatti-brouillard |website=Top Gear}}</ref><gallery mode=packed widths="220" heights="140">
</gallery>

=== F.K.P. Hommage ===
[[File:Bugatti F.K.P. Hommage (55088962018) (cropped).jpg|thumb|right|F.K.P. Hommage]]
In January 2026, Bugatti revealed its Programme Solitaire's second one-off model called the F.K.P. Hommage, which was named after the late [[Ferdinand Piëch|Ferdinand Karl Piëch]], as well as paying homage to the original [[Bugatti Veyron|Veyron]], specifically chassis no. 001, which was designed by Piëch. It is based on the Mistral's chassis and body, with the latter being heavily redesigned to be similar to that of the Veyron, albeit with some new details such as the headlights and exhausts. Like the Brouillard, it uses the same {{cvt|7993|cc|cuin L|0|abbr=on}} quad-turbocharged W16 engine from the [[Bugatti Chiron|Chiron Super Sport]], capable of producing {{cvt|1,600|PS|kW hp PS|0|order=out}}, which is more powerful than the car it was inspired by ({{cvt|1,001|hp|kW hp PS|0|order=out}}).<ref name=":0" /><ref name=":1" />

